From 888d666d80e3f879730112b5bae87a1ce34e7d02 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: K11MCH1 <81425424+K11MCH1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 09:33:58 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Proton: QueryPerformanceCounter reads the CPU timer on
 ARM64EC

Windows on ARM answers QueryPerformanceCounter from the generic timer
(cntvct_el0) without leaving user space. Wine's ARM64EC ntdll makes a system
call for every one, through FEX's syscall callback and Wine's dispatcher into
clock_gettime. DroidDeck-Proton's user-mode TSC patch excludes ARM64EC, so
phones still pay for every read.

droiddeck-qpc-timer points the Windows side's RtlQueryPerformanceCounter,
RtlQueryPerformanceFrequency (cntfrq_el0) and NtQueryPerformanceCounter stub
at the timer, in an ntdll.dll whose SHA-256 and original bytes it knows
(DroidDeck-Proton 11-11.9), keeping the Proton's file as ntdll.dll.orig.
Wine's Linux side keeps its own 100 ns counter. The Proton wrapper runs it
right after the component swaps, for game launches only, and it never fails
a launch.

From DDO Mobile, where Dungeons & Dragons Online's game thread spent 5.1% of
a world load reading the clock, and 0.06% with the timer.
